    "instruction": "Patient is a 68yo male on Warfarin 5mg daily, Fluconazole 200mg for 14 days, and Aspirin 81mg. Review for interactions.",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Warfarin (Vitamin K antagonist), Fluconazole (Azole antifungal), Aspirin (Antiplatelet/NSAID).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: Fluconazole is a potent CYP2C9 inhibitor; Warfarin is primarily metabolized by CYP2C9 (S-enantiomer) and CYP3A4.\n3. Pharmacodynamics: Inhibition of CYP2C9 reduces Warfarin clearance, raising INR. Aspirin adds pharmacodynamic antiplatelet effect and can cause GI mucosal injury.\n4. Net effect: Triple threat — elevated Warfarin exposure, impaired platelet function, and GI bleeding risk.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Severe bleeding risk from combined CYP2C9 inhibition and antiplatelet effect [94.0%]\n├── Supporting: Fluconazole consistently raises Warfarin AUC by 90–100%; Aspirin disrupts GI mucosa and platelet aggregation.\n└── Weakening: Aspirin dose is low (81mg); patient may be on cardioprotective indication that outweighs risk.\n\nInterpretation B: Manageable risk with close INR monitoring [6.0%]\n├── Supporting: Short 14-day Fluconazole course; provider may adjust Warfarin empirically.\n└── Weakening: Elderly male with likely comorbidities; lag in INR checks could be fatal.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: No clinically meaningful interaction. (CYP2C9 inhibition by Fluconazole is well-documented to nearly double Warfarin exposure; dismissing it is pharmacologically indefensible).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Fluconazole inhibits CYP2C9 and significantly increases Warfarin plasma levels, raising bleeding risk<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 Fluconazole potently inhibits CYP2C9, the primary metabolic pathway for S-Warfarin, causing a clinically dangerous rise in INR. Concurrent Aspirin further compounds hemorrhagic risk through antiplatelet and GI mucosal effects.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Reduce Warfarin dose empirically by 25–50% at Fluconazole initiation; check INR every 3–4 days during the antifungal course and 1 week after completion. Consider proton pump inhibitor co-therapy to protect GI mucosa. If Aspirin is not cardioprotectively mandatory, discuss temporary discontinuation with the prescribing cardiologist."
