    "instruction": "65yo male on Clopidogrel 75mg, Omeprazole 40mg, and Atorvastatin 80mg post-MI. Any concerns?",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Clopidogrel (Prodrug antiplatelet), Omeprazole (PPI, CYP2C19 inhibitor), Atorvastatin (Statin).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: Clopidogrel requires CYP2C19 bioactivation to its active thiol metabolite. Omeprazole is a potent CYP2C19 inhibitor, reducing active metabolite formation by 40–50%.\n3. Clinical consequence: Reduced platelet inhibition in a post-MI patient on dual antiplatelet therapy risks stent thrombosis.\n4. Atorvastatin at 80mg: Primarily CYP3A4 substrate; no significant interaction with Clopidogrel or Omeprazole at this pathway.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Omeprazole meaningfully attenuates Clopidogrel antiplatelet efficacy [80.0%]\n├── Supporting: FDA drug safety communication (2010) warns against this combination; platelet aggregation studies confirm reduced inhibition.\n└── Weakening: Clinical outcomes data (COGENT trial) did not show statistically significant increase in MACE, though it was underpowered.\n\nInterpretation B: GI protection outweighs theoretical antiplatelet attenuation [20.0%]\n├── Supporting: Post-MI patients on dual antiplatelet therapy have high GI bleed risk; PPI co-therapy reduces this.\n└── Weakening: Not all PPIs are equivalent CYP2C19 inhibitors; a less potent alternative exists.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Atorvastatin causes the primary interaction concern. (Atorvastatin does not affect CYP2C19; the statin-clopidogrel interaction via CYP3A4 is clinically negligible).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19 and reduces Clopidogrel activation, diminishing antiplatelet effect<|\"|>",
    "output": "🟡 Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19, the enzyme responsible for converting Clopidogrel to its active antiplatelet metabolite, potentially reducing its efficacy in a high-stakes post-MI setting.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Switch from Omeprazole to Pantoprazole or Rabeprazole, which have significantly weaker CYP2C19 inhibitory activity and do not meaningfully impair Clopidogrel activation. GI protection should be maintained given dual antiplatelet therapy. Atorvastatin 80mg requires no modification. Confirm the patient's CYP2C19 genotype if available, as poor metabolizers may need alternative antiplatelet therapy regardless of PPI choice."
