    "instruction": "Patient on Sertraline 100mg, Tramadol 50mg PRN, and Linezolid 600mg IV for MRSA. Evaluate for safety.",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Sertraline (SSRI), Tramadol (Opioid + serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor), Linezolid (Oxazolidinone antibiotic and non-selective MAO inhibitor).\n2. Serotonergic mechanisms: Sertraline inhibits serotonin reuptake; Tramadol inhibits serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake AND activates mu-opioid receptors; Linezolid inhibits MAO-A, reducing serotonin catabolism.\n3. Net effect: Triple serotonergic load — reuptake inhibition × 2 plus MAO inhibition — creates high probability of Serotonin Syndrome.\n4. Clinical urgency: Serotonin Syndrome can escalate to hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, seizures, and death.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Imminent life-threatening Serotonin Syndrome risk [97.0%]\n├── Supporting: Linezolid + SSRI combination is a well-documented cause of Serotonin Syndrome; adding Tramadol removes all pharmacological buffering.\n└── Weakening: Tramadol is PRN and may not be used concurrently; risk is dose/timing-dependent.\n\nInterpretation B: Risk is conditional on concurrent Tramadol use [3.0%]\n├── Supporting: If Tramadol is withheld during Linezolid course, the Sertraline + Linezolid risk is present but more manageable with monitoring.\n└── Weakening: Even the two-drug Sertraline + Linezolid combination carries FDA Black Box Warning.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: The primary concern is Linezolid-induced bone marrow suppression. (While myelosuppression is a class concern for prolonged Linezolid use, the acute serotonergic interaction is the immediate life-threatening priority).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Linezolid combined with SSRI and serotonergic opioids causes life-threatening Serotonin Syndrome<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 This is a life-threatening drug combination. Linezolid is a non-selective MAO inhibitor; combined with Sertraline (SSRI) and Tramadol (serotonin reuptake inhibitor + opioid), it creates overwhelming serotonergic excess consistent with Serotonin Syndrome.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: URGENT — Linezolid and Tramadol must not be co-administered with Sertraline. If Linezolid is essential for MRSA treatment, Sertraline must be held (allow ≥24 hours washout given drug dynamics, but note Sertraline has a short half-life) and Tramadol must be discontinued and replaced with a non-serotonergic analgesic (e.g., oxycodone or morphine with caution). Consider infectious disease consultation for alternative MRSA agents (e.g., Daptomycin, Vancomycin). Monitor for hyperthermia, clonus, agitation, and diaphoresis."
  },
