    "instruction": "73yo female on Digoxin 0.125mg, Amiodarone 200mg, and Clarithromycin 500mg BID for pneumonia. Is this safe?",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Digoxin (Cardiac glycoside), Amiodarone (Class III antiarrhythmic), Clarithromycin (Macrolide antibiotic).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: Digoxin is a P-glycoprotein (P-gp) substrate. Amiodarone inhibits both P-gp and CYP3A4. Clarithromycin is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor and P-gp inhibitor.\n3. Net effect: Dual P-gp and CYP inhibition raises Digoxin plasma concentrations significantly (Amiodarone alone raises Digoxin levels by 50–70%; Clarithromycin adds further inhibition).\n4. QT prolongation: Both Amiodarone and Clarithromycin independently prolong QTc; combination creates additive QT risk on top of Digoxin toxicity risk.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Severe Digoxin toxicity risk compounded by QT prolongation [95.0%]\n├── Supporting: Amiodarone is a well-documented Digoxin level elevator (package insert mandates 50% Digoxin dose reduction); Clarithromycin further inhibits elimination.\n└── Weakening: 0.125mg is already a low Digoxin dose, which may partially buffer the elevation.\n\nInterpretation B: Manageable with Digoxin dose reduction and monitoring [5.0%]\n├── Supporting: If Digoxin serum level is monitored frequently (every 2–3 days) and dose adjusted, toxicity may be averted.\n└── Weakening: Elderly patients have reduced renal clearance of Digoxin; narrow therapeutic index makes any elevation dangerous.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Clarithromycin is the sole concern and Amiodarone has no impact. (Amiodarone independently and substantially raises Digoxin levels; both mechanisms must be addressed simultaneously).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Amiodarone and Clarithromycin both inhibit P-glycoprotein causing Digoxin toxicity risk and QT prolongation<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 Critically dangerous combination: both Amiodarone and Clarithromycin inhibit P-glycoprotein and CYP3A4, dramatically increasing Digoxin plasma levels in a patient with already limited renal clearance. Additional QTc prolongation risk from Amiodarone + Clarithromycin combination amplifies cardiac arrhythmia risk.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Replace Clarithromycin with a non-QT-prolonging, non-P-gp-inhibiting antibiotic (e.g., Amoxicillin-Clavulanate or Doxycycline) if clinically appropriate for the pneumonia pathogen. If Clarithromycin must be used, reduce Digoxin dose by 50%, obtain a baseline serum Digoxin level, monitor ECG (QTc) daily, and recheck Digoxin level in 48–72 hours. Target Digoxin level 0.5–0.9 ng/mL. Obtain electrolytes (K⁺, Mg²⁺) and correct any deficits, as hypokalemia potentiates Digoxin toxicity."
  },
