    "instruction": "45yo female on Valproate 1000mg BID for epilepsy and started Lamotrigine 50mg BID as adjunct therapy. Dose review needed?",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Valproate (Anticonvulsant, UGT enzyme inhibitor), Lamotrigine (Anticonvulsant, UGT glucuronidation substrate).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: Lamotrigine is primarily eliminated by UGT1A4-mediated glucuronidation. Valproate inhibits UGT1A4, markedly reducing Lamotrigine clearance and raising plasma levels by approximately 2-fold.\n3. Clinical consequence: Standard Lamotrigine doses become supratherapeutic in the presence of Valproate, causing dose-dependent toxicity: diplopia, ataxia, dizziness, rash (including risk of Stevens-Johnson Syndrome/TEN, which is dose-rate dependent).\n4. Dosing guidance: When adding Lamotrigine to Valproate therapy, Lamotrigine starting doses and titration rates must be halved to avoid toxicity.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Current Lamotrigine dose is likely too high given Valproate co-therapy; dose reduction and slower titration required [94.0%]\n├── Supporting: Established pharmacokinetic interaction requires halved Lamotrigine doses with Valproate co-administration; 50mg BID is the standard starting dose WITHOUT Valproate — it is already 2× too high for initiation with Valproate.\n└── Weakening: Patient may already be at a stabilized level without acute toxicity symptoms; clinical assessment matters.\n\nInterpretation B: Current clinical response is the primary guide; pharmacokinetics alone should not dictate dosing [6.0%]\n├── Supporting: If seizure control is adequate and no toxicity symptoms are present, dose continuation could be justified with level monitoring.\n└── Weakening: SJS/TEN risk from Lamotrigine is dose-rate dependent early in therapy; initial weeks are the highest risk period for rash, and current dose is already double the recommended initiation dose with Valproate.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Lamotrigine inhibits Valproate metabolism, raising Valproate toxicity risk. (Lamotrigine does not significantly affect Valproate pharmacokinetics; the interaction is unidirectional — Valproate raises Lamotrigine levels, not vice versa).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Valproate inhibits UGT1A4 and doubles Lamotrigine plasma levels, requiring halved Lamotrigine doses during co-administration<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 Valproate is a potent UGT1A4 inhibitor that approximately doubles Lamotrigine plasma concentrations. Initiating Lamotrigine at 50mg BID in a patient already on Valproate is twice the recommended starting dose and significantly elevates the risk of serious rash (Stevens-Johnson Syndrome) and neurotoxicity.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Immediately reduce Lamotrigine to 25mg once daily for the first 2 weeks, then 25mg BID for weeks 3–4, with a maximum titration increase of 25–50mg every 1–2 weeks when co-administered with Valproate. The target maintenance dose with Valproate is typically 100–200mg/day (versus 300–500mg/day without Valproate). Monitor for any rash — any rash in the first 8 weeks requires urgent evaluation and likely Lamotrigine discontinuation. Obtain a Lamotrigine serum level if available. Educate the patient to report skin changes, mucosal lesions, or eye irritation immediately."
