    "instruction": "Patient on Cyclosporine 150mg BID (renal transplant) and prescribed Simvastatin 20mg for hypercholesterolemia. Review.",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Cyclosporine (Calcineurin inhibitor, CYP3A4 and P-gp inhibitor + OATP1B1 inhibitor), Simvastatin (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor, CYP3A4 substrate + OATP1B1 substrate).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: Cyclosporine is a potent inhibitor of CYP3A4, P-gp, and OATP1B1 — three major elimination pathways for Simvastatin. Simvastatin AUC increases approximately 6–8-fold with Cyclosporine co-administration.\n3. Toxicity risk: This magnitude of Simvastatin exposure increase creates near-certain dose-dependent myopathy and rhabdomyolysis risk.\n4. FDA guidance: Simvastatin is contraindicated in patients on Cyclosporine per the FDA-mandated label update following multiple rhabdomyolysis cases.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Absolute contraindication — Simvastatin must not be used with Cyclosporine [98.0%]\n├── Supporting: FDA labeling explicitly contraindicates Simvastatin with Cyclosporine; documented fatal rhabdomyolysis cases have occurred.\n└── Weakening: Transplant patients are at higher cardiovascular risk and need lipid management — the clinical need is real, but the drug choice must change.\n\nInterpretation B: Very low Simvastatin dose (5mg) might be tolerated with monitoring [2.0%]\n├── Supporting: Some older transplant protocols used very low-dose Simvastatin, though this predates the FDA contraindication update.\n└── Weakening: The FDA contraindication does not include dose exceptions; even low doses carry elevated myopathy risk given the magnitude of PK interaction.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Statin therapy is unnecessary in transplant patients who receive Cyclosporine. (Cyclosporine itself contributes to post-transplant dyslipidemia; statin therapy is often indicated in transplant recipients — the issue is statin selection, not statin use per se).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Cyclosporine inhibits CYP3A4, P-gp, and OATP1B1, causing 6–8-fold Simvastatin AUC increase and contraindicated co-administration<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 Absolute contraindication. Cyclosporine is a potent inhibitor of CYP3A4, P-glycoprotein, and OATP1B1 — increasing Simvastatin exposure by 6–8-fold. This combination is FDA-contraindicated due to documented fatal rhabdomyolysis cases.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Discontinue Simvastatin immediately. In Cyclosporine-treated transplant patients, the statin of choice is Fluvastatin (least CYP3A4 dependent, OATP1B1 interaction is more modest) at the lowest effective dose. Pravastatin is another option with acceptable interaction profile in Cyclosporine recipients (although Cyclosporine still raises Pravastatin levels ~5-fold via OATP1B1, Pravastatin's wider therapeutic index makes this manageable). Rosuvastatin should be used at ≤5mg/day. Monitor CK, LFTs, and serum creatinine 4–8 weeks after statin initiation in this population. Evaluate whether Tacrolimus (lower lipid interaction profile than Cyclosporine) could replace Cyclosporine in the immunosuppressive regimen."
