    "instruction": "69yo female on Allopurinol 300mg for gout and prescribed Azathioprine 150mg for newly diagnosed IBD. Evaluate.",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Allopurinol (Xanthine oxidase inhibitor), Azathioprine (Prodrug — metabolized via TPMT to 6-TGN and via xanthine oxidase to inactive 8-hydroxy-6-mercaptopurine).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: Azathioprine is converted to 6-Mercaptopurine (6-MP), which is catabolized by xanthine oxidase (XO) to inactive metabolites. Allopurinol inhibits XO, shunting 6-MP metabolism toward the TPMT pathway and increasing cytotoxic 6-TGN accumulation.\n3. Toxicity consequence: 6-TGN is the active cytotoxic metabolite responsible for bone marrow suppression (leukopenia, pancytopenia), hepatotoxicity, and infection risk. The combination can cause life-threatening myelosuppression.\n4. Standard management: When combination is unavoidable, Azathioprine dose must be reduced by 75% (i.e., to 25% of standard dose).\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Severe myelosuppression risk — Azathioprine dose must be reduced by 75% immediately [97.0%]\n├── Supporting: This is a major, well-characterized drug interaction taught in clinical pharmacology; at standard Azathioprine dose with Allopurinol, fatal pancytopenia has been documented.\n└── Weakening: Combination is sometimes intentionally used (the Sparrow protocol) in IBD specifically to increase 6-TGN levels for better thiopurine response — but always with deliberate 75% dose reduction.\n\nInterpretation B: Alternative gout medication should be used to preserve standard Azathioprine dosing [3.0%]\n├── Supporting: Switching from Allopurinol to Febuxostat (also a XO inhibitor — same interaction risk) or to a uricosuric agent (Probenecid — does not inhibit XO) avoids the pharmacokinetic interaction.\n└── Weakening: Probenecid has its own interactions and is less used; Febuxostat carries the same XO inhibition problem.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Interaction only occurs with 6-Mercaptopurine, not Azathioprine. (Azathioprine is a prodrug of 6-MP; the XO inhibition interaction applies identically to Azathioprine and 6-MP — it is the same biochemical pathway).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase and causes profound accumulation of Azathioprine's cytotoxic 6-TGN metabolite, requiring 75% dose reduction<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 Life-threatening interaction. Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, the enzyme that catabolizes Azathioprine's active metabolite 6-TGN to inactive byproducts. This causes cytotoxic 6-TGN accumulation, leading to potentially fatal bone marrow suppression and severe myelosuppression.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: If both drugs are clinically required: immediately reduce Azathioprine to 37.5mg (25% of 150mg) and monitor CBC with differential weekly for the first 4 weeks, then every 2 weeks. Check TPMT genotype/phenotype before proceeding — TPMT-deficient patients have even greater 6-TGN accumulation risk. Consider switching Allopurinol to Probenecid (a uricosuric agent that does not inhibit XO) if appropriate for the patient's gout management (check for renal function and uric acid overproduction vs. underexcretion). Alternatively, the Sparrow protocol (deliberate low-dose Azathioprine + Allopurinol in IBD) can be considered only with explicit specialist oversight, TPMT testing, and intensive hematologic monitoring."
  },
