    "instruction": "70yo male on Apixaban 5mg BID and St. John's Wort 300mg TID (self-initiated herbal supplement). Assess.",
    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Apixaban (Factor Xa inhibitor DOAC, CYP3A4 and P-gp substrate), St. John's Wort (Herbal extract — active component Hyperforin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and P-gp).\n2. Pharmacokinetics: St. John's Wort is a potent CYP3A4 and P-gp inducer; it significantly reduces Apixaban plasma concentrations by increasing both intestinal efflux (P-gp) and hepatic/intestinal metabolism (CYP3A4).\n3. Clinical consequence: Reduced Apixaban exposure in an AF patient risks inadequate anticoagulation, with increased thromboembolic (stroke) risk.\n4. Herb-drug interaction awareness: St. John's Wort is frequently self-administered by patients without disclosing it to healthcare providers; systematic screening for supplement use is essential.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: St. John's Wort significantly reduces Apixaban anticoagulant protection — must be discontinued [91.0%]\n├── Supporting: St. John's Wort reduces Apixaban AUC by approximately 54% — more than halving anticoagulant exposure; FDA labeling for Apixaban warns against this combination.\n└── Weakening: CYP3A4 induction typically reaches maximum effect at 1–2 weeks; if newly initiated, maximal Apixaban reduction may not yet have occurred.\n\nInterpretation B: Monitoring anti-Xa activity could guide management [9.0%]\n├── Supporting: Anti-Xa activity assays calibrated for Apixaban can confirm subtherapeutic levels.\n└── Weakening: Anti-Xa monitoring is not routinely available or standardized for clinical decision-making; discontinuing the herb is simpler and safer.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: St. John's Wort has no clinically relevant interactions with anticoagulants. (This is a dangerous misconception; St. John's Wort's CYP3A4/P-gp induction is clinically documented to reduce DOAC, Warfarin, and multiple other drug plasma levels significantly).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>St. John's Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, reducing Apixaban plasma concentrations by approximately 54%<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 St. John's Wort (Hyperforin) is a potent CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein inducer that reduces Apixaban anticoagulant exposure by ~54%, potentially leaving the patient undertreated for atrial fibrillation and at substantially increased stroke risk.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Counsel the patient to discontinue St. John's Wort immediately. CYP3A4 induction reverses over 1–4 weeks after discontinuation; monitor for bleeding signs as Apixaban levels may rise post-discontinuation. Screen all patients on DOACs, Warfarin, antiepileptics, antiretrovirals, and transplant medications for concurrent herbal supplement use at every medication review. Document this interaction in the patient's chart as a safety alert. If the patient requires treatment for mood disorder (a common use of St. John's Wort), refer to a mental health provider for appropriate pharmacotherapy."
