    "thought_process": "[Logical Chain]\n1. Target drugs: Warfarin (Vitamin K antagonist, CYP2C9/1A2/3A4 substrate), Acetaminophen (Analgesic/antipyretic).\n2. Proposed mechanism: High-dose regular acetaminophen (>2g/day for multiple days) can potentiate Warfarin's anticoagulant effect. The proposed mechanisms include inhibition of Vitamin K-dependent carboxylation or CYP-mediated Warfarin metabolite interaction — though the exact mechanism remains debated.\n3. Clinical data: Multiple clinical studies and case series have confirmed that regular acetaminophen >2g/day is associated with INR elevation and increased bleeding in Warfarin patients; single or occasional doses appear safe.\n4. Comparative context: Acetaminophen remains the preferred analgesic in Warfarin patients because it avoids the platelet dysfunction and GI mucosal injury of NSAIDs — but the dose-dependent Warfarin potentiation must be recognized.\n\n[Competing Hypotheses]\nInterpretation A: Regular high-dose Acetaminophen potentiates Warfarin and requires more frequent INR monitoring [80.0%]\n├── Supporting: Consistent pharmacodynamic potentiation documented in RCTs and observational studies; clinically significant INR elevation with doses >2g/day regular use.\n└── Weakening: Effect is modest compared to NSAIDs or CYP inhibitors; at 3g/day (within standard maximum), some patients experience no significant INR change.\n\nInterpretation B: This dose is within safe limits for Warfarin-treated patients [20.0%]\n├── Supporting: ACC and clinical guidelines cite acetaminophen as the preferred analgesic in anticoagulated patients; most practitioners consider ≤3g/day acceptable.\n└── Weakening: 'Preferred' does not mean 'no interaction'; enhanced INR monitoring is still warranted with regular use.\n\n[Discarded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Acetaminophen has no relevant interaction with Warfarin at any dose. (This misconception leads to INR lability in Warfarin-managed patients; while Acetaminophen is safer than NSAIDs, the dose-dependent INR potentiation at regular high doses is pharmacologically real).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Regular high-dose Acetaminophen (>2g/day) potentiates Warfarin anticoagulant effect with risk of INR elevation<|\"|>",
    "output": "🟡 Regular daily Acetaminophen at ≥2g/day can potentiate Warfarin's anticoagulant effect, causing INR elevation. While Acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic alternative to NSAIDs in anticoagulated patients, dose-dependent interaction at 3g/day warrants monitoring.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Continue Acetaminophen as the preferred analgesic (superior to NSAIDs in Warfarin patients due to absent platelet effect and GI mucosal safety). Limit Acetaminophen to ≤2g/day when possible. Check INR within 5–7 days of initiating or increasing regular Acetaminophen use and adjust Warfarin accordingly. Educate the patient on acetaminophen content in OTC combination products (cold/flu medications, sleep aids) to prevent inadvertent overdosing. Maintain a consistent daily Acetaminophen dose rather than variable PRN use to stabilize INR. If arthritis pain is poorly controlled at ≤2g/day, consider topical diclofenac or brief glucocorticoid injections."
  },
