m is alpha-2 mediated, not beta-mediated; beta-blockers do not block Clonidine's pharmacodynamic effect — they specifically create risk during Clonidine discontinuation by leaving alpha receptors unopposed).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Abrupt Clonidine discontinuation while on beta-blocker causes unopposed alpha-adrenergic rebound leading to hypertensive crisis<|\"|>",
    "output": "🟡 The Clonidine-Metoprolol combination is pharmacodynamically acceptable during stable therapy but carries a critical safety risk: if Clonidine is abruptly discontinued, beta-blockade leaves alpha-adrenergic receptors unopposed to catecholamine surge, precipitating a hypertensive crisis.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Educate the patient extensively — never stop Clonidine abruptly and ensure adequate supply at all times. If transitioning off this combination, taper Metoprolol first over 1–2 weeks, then slowly taper Clonidine over a subsequent 1–2 weeks (reduce by 0.1mg every 3–7 days). If Clonidine was accidentally missed or ran out, treat as potential medical emergency — restart Clonidine immediately and monitor blood pressure closely. Consider transitioning to a single antihypertensive agent with a lower risk profile if adherence is a concern. Provide the patient with written instructions about the withdrawal risk."
