tinued before starting Sacubitril/Valsartan. (Beta-blockers are maintained and are part of optimal HFrEF background therapy alongside ARNIs; there is no contraindication to their combination — in fact, they are synergistic for HF outcomes).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Sacubitril/Valsartan initiation in HFrEF with background Furosemide and Bisoprolol carries hypotension risk requiring careful uptitration and the mandatory 36-hour ACEi washout if applicable<|\"|>",
    "output": "🟡 Sacubitril/Valsartan initiation in an elderly HFrEF patient on Furosemide and Bisoprolol carries clinically significant first-dose and early-treatment hypotension risk. Additionally, if transitioning from an ACE inhibitor, the mandatory 36-hour washout must be confirmed to prevent life-threatening angioedema.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Confirm ACEi washout: if the patient was on an ACE inhibitor (Lisinopril, Ramipril, Enalapril, etc.), ensure ≥36 hours have elapsed since the last ACEi dose before the first Sacubitril/Valsartan dose. If transitioning from an ARB, no washout is required. Reduce Furosemide dose by 25–50% at ARNI initiation if the patient appears euvolemic to reduce hypotension risk. Check blood pressure (standing and sitting) before and 2 hours after the first dose. Recheck renal function and electrolytes (K+, Cr) at 1–2 weeks. Uptitrate Sacubitril/Valsartan to target 97/103mg BID over 2–4 weeks as tolerated. Maintain Bisoprolol — it is guideline-recommended as background HFrEF therapy alongside ARNIs."
