t; gout management must be adapted around chemotherapy, not vice versa — appropriate gout management during 5-FU therapy might include colchicine at adjusted doses or corticosteroids rather than Allopurinol initiation).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase and may modestly reduce 5-FU catabolism potentially increasing exposure during chemotherapy<|\"|>",
    "output": "🟡 Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase, a minor pathway in 5-FU catabolism, potentially modestly increasing 5-FU exposure and toxicity — though the primary 5-FU elimination pathway (DPD) is unaffected. The clinical significance is debated but the oncology team must be aware.\n\nConfidence: ⚠️ MODERATE\n\nRecommendation: Notify the oncology team of concurrent Allopurinol use before and during 5-FU treatment cycles. Monitor for enhanced 5-FU toxicity signs: mucositis, myelosuppression (CBC with differential), and GI toxicity. For acute gout flare during chemotherapy, consider Colchicine at renally-adjusted dose (preferred) or short-course corticosteroids as alternatives to initiating Allopurinol during active chemotherapy. If Allopurinol is essential for chronic tophaceous gout management, document the interaction risk in the oncology record and ensure enhanced toxicity monitoring during 5-FU infusion days and the 2-week post-infusion window. DPD enzyme testing (DPYD genotyping) should be confirmed before any 5-FU therapy regardless of Allopurinol use."
