arded Paths]\n✗ Discarded: Furosemide increases Vancomycin renal excretion and reduces plasma levels, decreasing ototoxicity risk. (The net pharmacokinetic effect of Furosemide is volume depletion → reduced GFR → impaired Vancomycin clearance → higher plasma levels; Furosemide does not accelerate Vancomycin elimination).\n\n▶ Selected: Interpretation A\n<|tool_call>verify_claim<|\"|>Vancomycin and Furosemide cause synergistic ototoxicity and Furosemide-induced volume depletion reduces Vancomycin renal clearance causing drug accumulation<|\"|>",
    "output": "🔴 Vancomycin and Furosemide have synergistic ototoxicity, and Furosemide-induced volume depletion reduces GFR and Vancomycin renal clearance, causing drug accumulation and amplifying both nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity risk.\n\nConfidence: ✅ HIGH\n\nRecommendation: Implement AUC-guided Vancomycin therapeutic drug monitoring (target AUC 400–600 mg·h/L per ASHP/IDSA/SIDP 2020 guidelines) rather than trough-based monitoring; this approach has been shown to reduce nephrotoxicity. Monitor SCr and BUN every 48–72 hours; adjust Vancomycin dosing interval (rather than dose size) for renal function changes. Maintain euvolemia with careful Furosemide titration — avoid excessive diuresis that reduces renal perfusion. Perform baseline audiometry and repeat if ototoxicity symptoms emerge (tinnitus, hearing change, vertigo). Consider switching to Furosemide alternatives (e.g., bumetanide) only if hypersensitivity to Furosemide exists — the drug class effect on ototoxicity persists. Infectious disease pharmacy consultation for Vancomycin dosing optimization is strongly recommended."
